title = Forks, mirrors, and merging across namespaces
mode = platform_theory

[diagram]

[code]
   canonical repo
        │
   fork │  (same forge or mirror from GitHub/GitLab)
        ▼
   contributor clone ──► PR to upstream
        │
        └──── mirror job keeps fork fresh (cron)
[/code]

[center][color=#00aa90]organization / team permissions[/color]  inherit like folders[/center]

[description]

[b]Forks[/b] duplicate refs and objects into another owner space so push access does not imply trust on the original repo. Gitea’s UI creates the fork and suggests remotes; Git operations match other forges.

[b]Pull from upstream:[/b] merge or rebase upstream default into your fork’s default locally, or use the web “Sync” where available—conflicts resolve with normal Git tools.

[b]Mirrors as collaboration aid:[/b] read-only mirrors of upstream can live beside active development repos; ensure issue trackers do not diverge confusingly between mirror and canonical.

[b]Organizations:[/b] group repos and delegate admin; watch default visibility (public vs private) when repos move between orgs.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] lightweight forges still need heavyweight habits: signed commits, small PRs, and explicit ownership of who may merge to production branches.
